WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.359
When people talk about actors undergoing physical
transformations for a role, they often focus

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:08.349
on stars who put on a bunch of weight, or
work out a ton.

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:10.080
But acting ain't just about the body.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.660
In fact, an actor's most important feature
is his or her face.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.939
Ever since the dawn of cinema, movie stars
have been using makeup to alter their appearance.

00:00:17.939 --> 00:00:22.679
And with the help of a fake nose or an artificial
forehead, an actor can transform into a completely

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:23.880
different being altogether.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.020
Here are some of the most memorable examples.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.240
Tilda Swinton, Snowpiercer

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.730
In addition to having truckloads of talent,
Tilda Swinton is something of a shape-shifter,

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:37.309
and for our money, her most radical facial
transformation was for the Korean thriller

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:37.880
Snowpiercer.

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:43.019
The film sees the actress play the nefarious
Minister Mason, a grotesque politician with

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.700
some of the ugliest teeth in cinema.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.220
Originally, the part was written for a man,
but director Bong Joon-Ho really wanted Swinton

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.460
to try the part on for size.

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:55.039
So with the help of makeup designer Jeremy
Woodhead, Swinton created a uniquely ugly

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:55.540
character.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.300
Really, the makeup seems so simple.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:58.500
A pair of dentures.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.540
A floppy wig.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:00.420
A prosthetic nose.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.260
But if the actress had gotten her way, Minister
Mason would've looked even worse.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.880
According to the director, Swinton originally
wanted the slimy spokesperson to sport a pig's

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:09.380
nose.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.740
But he explained that was just a bit "too
much," even for Snowpiercer.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.380
Halle Berry, Cloud Atlas

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:21.180
2012's Cloud Atlas is a wildly ambitious sci-fi
flick of epic proportions from the acclaimed

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.540
directing team of Lana and Lilly Wachowski.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.280
The film follows a select group of souls as
they continually cross paths across time,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.460
with various actors undergoing radical transformations,
changing race and gender from scene to scene.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.640
The decision was controversial, with critics
arguing over whether the movie was racially

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.920
insensitive, or progressive.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.480
But regardless of how you feel about the Wachowski's
vision, you have to admire the dedication

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:48.310
of the actors, with Halle Berry absolutely
disappearing into not one, but six different

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:48.800
roles.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.000
Over the course of nearly three hours, Berry
plays a grand total of six roles, each one

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.480
radically different from the last.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.560
While she impresses with all of her performances,
the two that truly stand out are a German-Jewish

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.740
woman and an ancient Korean man.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.130
To play the German woman in 1936, Berry underwent
eight hours of intensive work, which involved

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.140
everything from a prosthetic nose to skin
lightening treatments.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.880
But her most memorable appearance is definitely
the ghoulish Korean doctor in 2144.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.740
She sat in the makeup chair for six hours,
and while she had a lot of fun with this transformation,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.720
she told Entertainment Weekly that the teeth
were "just horrific."

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:25.960
The cast of Foxcatcher

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.220
When Foxcatcher hit theaters in 2014, everybody
noticed Steve Carell's schnoz.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.820
The actor drew quite a bit of flack for his
fake nose, but he really did look almost identical

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.460
to real-life murderer John du Pont.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.240
And the nose is just the tip of the cosmetics
iceberg when it comes to Foxcatcher.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.200
Makeup artist Bill Corso also masked Carell's
eyebrows with a prosthetic forehead, lightened

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.300
his skin tone, and even built him a fake lip.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.600
To top everything off, Corso gave Carell a
pair of dark contact lenses that really helped

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.200
him slip into the role of an obsessive multimillionaire.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.780
But while Carell gets all the attention, Channing
Tatum and Mark Ruffalo also underwent some

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.200
pretty impressive facial transformations to
look like genuine wrestlers.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:08.100
Tatum was given fake cauliflower ears and
a bridge to enlarge his nose, and his nostrils

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.080
and lips were widened with special prosthetics
called plumpers.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.900
Similarly, Ruffalo had prosthetics stuck behind
his upper lip and underwent frequent haircuts

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.320
to capture wrestler Dave Schultz's balding
hairline.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:21.820
He also had little brackets placed in the
back of his ears to make them stick out so

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:23.060
that he looked more like Tatum.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.400
Gary Oldman, Hannibal

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.740
Hannibal Lecter is one of the most famous
cinematic serial killers of all time, but

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.460
he isn't the only murderer stalking his franchise.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:37.220
In the 2001 film Hannibal, the sinister Mason
Verger is one of the few people who've survived

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.540
an encounter with the liver-eating psychiatrist.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.530
Of course, he didn't escape from Lecter totally
intact, as the sophisticated killer absolutely

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.140
demolished Verger's face.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:50.440
So when it came time to put script to screen,
makeup artist Greg Cannom needed to turn actor

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.600
Gary Oldman into a veritable monster.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.660
Using a special translucent silicone, Cannom
transformed Oldman into a lipless monster,

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.030
covered in scars and putrefied flesh.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.000
The whole ghoulish process took around six
hours, but perhaps the best touch came from

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.360
the mind of Oldman himself.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.770
The actor wanted to give Verger an eye that
was permanently opened, so Cannom put together

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:14.610
a setup involving a silk cloth, Velcro, and
a little wire that prevented Oldman's eyelids

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:15.280
from shutting.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:19.900
He also inserted a nasty looking contact lens
and had someone stand by with eye drops to

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.520
keep the actor from losing his vision.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:22.480
Yikes.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:24.900
Glenn Close, Albert Nobbs

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.939
The story of a Victorian-era woman living
as a man, 2011's Albert Nobbs was an incredibly

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:32.540
personal film for Glenn Close.

00:04:32.909 --> 00:04:37.140
She'd first played the role back in 1982 in
an off-Broadway show, and she'd wanted to

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.389
share the story on the silver screen for years.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:43.539
To pull it off, she needed to disguise her
face to inhabit her dream role, so she turned

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.880
to makeup expert Matthew Mungle for a helping
hand.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.300
Mungle wanted the makeup to be incredibly
subtle.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.260
He based the design largely off a photo that
Close had found of a "manly woman from the

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:52.760
'30s."

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.300
Working with prosthetic pieces and dental
plumpers, he kept things relatively simple,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.660
which he claims is a lot more difficult than
it sounds.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.279
He told the Los Angeles Times,

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.120
"To put a nose on someone's face and blend
it into their skin and make it look natural

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.640
is harder than wearing a whole mask."

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.400
So with just a few touches here and there,
Close stepped out of the makeup chair a new

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:10.900
man.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.260
In fact, when she finally looked in the mirror,
she was so overwhelmed that she began crying.

